body:
I don't mind the direction of expansion thing, that is easy fixed in a project. What I do think is a problem is that these stretching modes are introducing black bars horizontally and vertically when they could be doing neither. `keep_height` is intended to stretch as wide as it possible and get rid of the black bars on the sides (which it does in fractional scaling mode) but it introduces black bars on the sides anyways in integer mode. It could be avoided if it picked the scaling factor first, then calculated the viewport size. 

This behavior works well for fractional scaling. For people who use integer scaling to make pixel-art style games, I don't think any of the stretch aspect options produce desirable results. Often times these settings give black bars on all sides when it could be avoided if the settings worked better. I think the only useful option for integer scaling currently is the stretch aspect `keep` if you want black bars on both sides to maintain a fixed aspect ratio.

In my screenshots I worked around the problem with a global autoload script that automatically sets the zoom-factor based on the current resolution of the window and the base size from the settings. It's not doing anything with other nodes than the root window.
```gdscript
extends Node

@onready var root: Window = get_tree().root

func _ready() -> void:
	root.size_changed.connect(_resized)
	_resized()

func _resized() -> void:
	var ratios: Vector2 = Vector2(root.size) / Vector2(root.content_scale_size);
	var zoom: float = min(floor(ratios.x), floor(ratios.y))
	if zoom >= 1:
		root.content_scale_factor = zoom
```

Unfortunately a very similar problem occurs for `keep_width` and `keep_height` Stretch Aspects, so I think the logic for all of them may be a bit broken.
